# C#/.NET security

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic security rules to
C#/.NET-specific, OWASP-relevant risks and the safe framework API to use
instead. Applies only to `*.cs` files.

## SQL and data access

- Use parameterized queries for any SQL built from user-influenced
  input: `SqlCommand.Parameters.Add(...)`/`AddWithValue(...)` with `@`
  placeholders, or an ORM's parameterized query API (EF Core LINQ,
  Dapper's parameter objects) — never `string.Format`/interpolation/
  concatenation to build `CommandText` with a value that came from a
  caller. `$"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Name = '{name}'"` is a SQL
  injection sink even when it "looks like" it is just formatting.
- An ORM's raw-SQL escape hatch (EF Core's `FromSqlRaw`, Dapper's raw
  string execution) carries the same rule: pass user-influenced values
  as parameters, never interpolated directly into the raw SQL string.
  Prefer `FromSqlInterpolated`, which parameterizes `$"..."`
  interpolation holes automatically, over `FromSqlRaw` when the query
  needs any caller-supplied value at all.

## Deserialization of untrusted data

- Never use `BinaryFormatter` to deserialize data from any source you do
  not fully trust (or at all, on a current .NET version) — it is banned
  by Microsoft for security reasons because a crafted payload can
  execute arbitrary code during deserialization, and it is obsolete/
  removed on modern .NET.
- Deserialize untrusted JSON with `System.Text.Json` using an explicit,
  known target type — avoid `TypeNameHandling`-style polymorphic
  deserialization from an untrusted source (whether via
  `Newtonsoft.Json`'s `TypeNameHandling.Auto`/`All` or a custom
  `$type`-driven resolver), which lets the payload itself choose what
  type gets constructed.
- Validate/allowlist any type passed to `XmlSerializer`/`DataContractSerializer`
  from untrusted input the same way; do not resolve a type name found
  inside the untrusted payload without an allowlist.

## Secrets and configuration

- Never hard-code a connection string, API key, or signing secret in
  source or commit it in `appsettings.json` — use User Secrets
  (`dotnet user-secrets`) for local development and environment
  variables, Azure Key Vault, or another secret store the project has
  already standardized on for anything beyond local dev.
- A connection string or secret read from `IConfiguration` should come
  from an environment-specific provider (environment variables, a
  secret manager), not a value checked into `appsettings.json` or
  `appsettings.Development.json` that happens to work.

## Path and file handling

- Never build a filesystem path for an untrusted, caller-supplied
  filename/segment with plain string concatenation and open it directly
  — validate the resulting path stays under the intended root (e.g.
  resolve with `Path.GetFullPath` and confirm it starts with the root's
  full path, or use an allowlist of known filenames) before calling
  `File.Open`/`File.ReadAllText`/etc.; `Path.Combine` alone does not stop
  a `..`-containing segment from climbing outside the intended
  directory.

## Web and transport (ASP.NET Core)

- Never render user-influenced content into an HTML response outside of
  Razor's default auto-encoding (`Html.Raw`/`@Html.Raw` on
  caller-supplied content is an XSS vector) — let the framework's
  default encoding do its job unless the content is provably
  pre-sanitized.
- Never disable TLS certificate validation
  (`ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback` returning `true`
  unconditionally, or `HttpClientHandler.ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback`
  bypassing validation) outside a short-lived local test, and never ship
  it that way.
- Use the framework's built-in antiforgery support
  (`[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]`/`IAntiforgery`) for any state-changing
  endpoint reachable from a browser session, and rely on ASP.NET Core
  Identity/`PasswordHasher<TUser>` (or an equivalent vetted library) for
  password hashing — never a custom hash/roll-your-own scheme.
